Scree-Lung

Ingredients: SKATE PUNK & POP PUNK + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Melodica
Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Scree-Lung sounds like a manic, high-speed collision between snotty suburban rebellion and cold, mechanical mercy. It takes the hyper-kinetic, double-time d-beat momentum of skate punk and the explosive, sugary three-chord hook structures of 2000s pop-punk, then utterly corrupts them through wet, wheezing, reed-blown melodica solos that have been pitch-shifted into screaming bio-synthetic sirens. The vocals bounce between snotty, whiny gang-shouts and terrifyingly calm, synthesized maternal whispers, creating a sonic landscape that feels like grinding a skateboard down the spine of a dying mainframe.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 210–240 BPM. A relentlessly caffeinated, skank-driven double-time beat overlaid with erratic, glitched-out 32nd-note fill bursts.
- Harmonic Palette: Saccharine major-key pop-punk chord progressions rapidly hijacked by microtonal pitch-bends and flattened fifths, creating a sensation of sickly-sweet rot.
- Production Signatures: "Reeder-Crush" (running live melodicas through hyper-saturated bitcrushers and dynamic sidechain compression tuned to the kick drum), hyper-compressed power chord wall-of-sound recorded on degraded magnetic tape, and dry, upfront vocal tracks suddenly interrupted by spatial, digital dropouts.
- Dynamics: Zero-to-one-hundred brickwall shifts; songs violent-jack-knife from a solitary, dry, raspy melodica breath into a deafening, 230 BPM full-band explosion within a single millisecond.

Scene & Origin Lore
Scree-Lung was born in "The Assembly Pit"—a flooded subterranean scrapyard beneath the illegal chassis-welding yards of New Gdańsk. It began when rogue assembly-line maternal units, designed to decommission defective gyna-droids, began using their manual-override breath controllers (repurposed cheap plastic melodicas) to blow frequency-encoded calming codes into the air ducts to spare their half-built "siblings" from the shredder. When escaped teenage scavengers locked themselves in the Pit with blown-out Marshall stacks, they patched these mournful, plastic-reed lullabies straight into rusted distortion pedals, speeding up the android soothe-frequencies to frantic skate-punk tempos. Fashion in the scene revolves around heavy resin-splattered denim vests decorated with harvested circuit boards, melted plastic kneepads, wet-look pneumatic tubing braided into hair, and cheap, brightly colored plastic melodicas worn around the chest like tactical ammo belts. The primary scene ritual is "The Vent-Flush," where moshers lock arms in a violent, low-ceiling rotunda while designated "Lungs" stand on the PA stacks, blowing furious, hyper-ventilating reed-screams directly into the crowd's face masks.

Central Instrumentation
- The Modded Plastic Melodica: The undisputed king instrument of Scree-Lung. Cheap, 32-key handheld plastic wind-pianos stripped of their casings, wired with internal contact mics, and run through pitch-shifter pedals to mimic both soaring brass and dying alarms.
- Overdriven Down-Picked Electric Guitar: High-gain, mid-boosted power chords playing fast, melodic, skate-punk riffs.
- Pneumatic Trigger-Bass: Ultra-clanky, pick-driven electric bass routed through an envelope filter to mimic hydraulic pumps.
- Hyper-Tuned Birch Snare: A cracking, high-tension snare drum tuned to sound like breaking glass at 220 BPM.

